From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] setup.py: wipe the stale _skbuild cache before each
 invocation

Upstream-Status: Issue [https://github.com/scikit-build/scikit-build/issues/521]

`python -m build` runs this file twice per wheel: once for
`prepare_metadata_for_build_wheel`, once for `build_wheel`, each inside
its own isolated build venv with a different path. Both invocations
share the same on-disk `_skbuild/<plat>-<machine>-<pyver>/cmake-build`
directory (computed deterministically by `get_skbuild_dir()`), and
`run_conan_install()` only `os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True)`s it rather
than clearing it, so the second invocation's CMake configure reuses the
first invocation's leftover `CMakeCache.txt` and generated
`*.cmake` files, which still point at the first invocation's
(now-deleted) venv.

On riscv64 this makes the second configure fail with:

    CMake-installed files must be within the project root.
      Project Root:
        .../_skbuild/linux-riscv64-3.12/cmake-install
      Violating Files:
        .../_skbuild/linux-riscv64-3.12/cmake-build/python/resdata/.bin/rd_pack.x
        ...

scikit-build's own `check_for_bad_installs()` parses the stale
`*.cmake` files looking for a `${CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX}` placeholder to
substitute; the first invocation's now-inconsistent cache entries no
longer carry one, so the raw path it finds is the build directory
itself, which trivially fails the "is this inside the install
directory" check. This is exactly scikit-build/scikit-build#521
("pip install . should invalidate CMake cache in temporary
directory"), filed in 2020 and closed `not planned` in July 2026 with
no fix landing in classic scikit-build (the maintainer pointed
reporters at scikit-build-core instead, which this project does not
use). Other scikit-build projects hit by the same bug (NIMPA, NIPET,
arcae, tiktaalik) all carry the same workaround upstream never did:
delete the stale cache themselves before configuring.

Wipe `_skbuild/<plat>-<machine>-<pyver>/cmake-build` at the top of this
file, before `run_conan_install()` ever populates it, so every
invocation of `setup.py` -- metadata or wheel, first or second --
always starts from a clean configure instead of a stale one.

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
 setup.py | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/setup.py b/setup.py
index f149797..5b45931 100644
--- a/setup.py
+++ b/setup.py
@@ -99,6 +99,18 @@
     return cmake_args
 
 
+# scikit-build (classic) reuses this same _skbuild/<plat>/cmake-build directory
+# across the separate metadata-prep and wheel-build invocations that `python -m
+# build` makes of this file, each in its own isolated venv with a different
+# path. The CMake cache left behind by the earlier invocation doesn't match
+# the new one's paths, which trips skbuild's own "CMake-installed files must
+# be within the project root" check (scikit-build/scikit-build#521, closed
+# not-planned). Wipe it before each invocation so every build starts from a
+# clean configure.
+import shutil
+
+shutil.rmtree(get_skbuild_dir(), ignore_errors=True)
+
 skbuild_dir = run_conan_install()
 CMAKE_ARGS_FROM_PRESET = get_cmake_args_from_preset(skbuild_dir)
 
